{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.Mathlib.EllipticDivisorDegree","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.Mathlib.EllipticDivisorDegree","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:Mathlib.Analysis.Meromorphic.Divisor","lean:module:Mathlib.Analysis.Meromorphic.Order","lean:module:Mathlib.Analysis.Calculus.LogDeriv","lean:module:Mathlib.Analysis.Calculus.Deriv.Shift","lean:module:Mathlib.Analysis.Complex.Basic","lean:module:Mathlib.Algebra.Ring.Periodic","lean:module:Mathlib.LinearAlgebra.LinearIndependent.Defs","lean:module:Mathlib.Data.Fin.VecNotation"],"declarations":15,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mathlib.EllipticDivisorDegree.elliptic_divisor_degree_zero","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mathlib.EllipticDivisorDegree.elliptic_divisor_degree_zero","kind":"theorem","line":220,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/EllipticDivisorDegree.lean#L220-L247","doc":"**Liouville's third theorem: the degree of an elliptic divisor is zero.**\nFor a nonconstant elliptic function `f` with `ℝ`-independent periods, the sum of\nthe local orders (zeros minus poles, with multiplicity) over one fundamental\nparallelogram vanishes. `S` enumerates the finitely many divisor points inside the\ncell (the finiteness — support locally finite ∩ compact cell — is a separate,\ntractable brick; it is passed as `hS` so that the analytic core is isolated).\n\n**OPEN.** The classical proof is\n`Σ_{p∈P} ord_p f = (1/2πi) ∮_{∂P} logDeriv f = 0`, where the contour integral\nvanishes because `logDeriv f` is elliptic (`isElliptic_logDeriv`, proved above) so\nopposite edges cancel. The first equality is the **argument principle**, which is\nabsent from Mathlib v4.24.0 (no elliptic-function theory, no residue theorem). The\nboundary-cancellation half is formalized here; the argument-principle half is the\ngenuine gap. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mathlib.EllipticDivisorDegree.HasArgumentPrinciple.argument_principle_parallelogram","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mathlib.EllipticDivisorDegree.HasArgumentPrinciple.argument_principle_parallelogram","kind":"theorem","line":218,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/EllipticDivisorDegree.lean#L218-L218","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mathlib.EllipticDivisorDegree.HasArgumentPrinciple.mk","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mathlib.EllipticDivisorDegree.HasArgumentPrinciple.mk","kind":"ctor","line":217,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/EllipticDivisorDegree.lean#L217-L217","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mathlib.EllipticDivisorDegree.HasArgumentPrinciple","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mathlib.EllipticDivisorDegree.HasArgumentPrinciple","kind":"class","line":215,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/EllipticDivisorDegree.lean#L215-L218","doc":"**The argument principle for a fundamental parallelogram.**\nCarried as a class since Mathlib lacks contour integrals over parallelograms. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mathlib.EllipticDivisorDegree.divisor_translate","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mathlib.EllipticDivisorDegree.divisor_translate","kind":"theorem","line":202,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/EllipticDivisorDegree.lean#L202-L211","doc":"**The divisor is lattice-periodic.** For `f` meromorphic on `ℂ` and periodic\nunder `c`, `(divisor f).(z+c) = (divisor f).z`. So `Σ_cell ord` over any one\nfundamental parallelogram is the full \"degree over a fundamental domain\", and the\ndegree-zero theorem below is a statement about a single cell. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mathlib.EllipticDivisorDegree.meromorphicOrderAt_translate","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mathlib.EllipticDivisorDegree.meromorphicOrderAt_translate","kind":"theorem","line":191,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/EllipticDivisorDegree.lean#L191-L200","doc":"**The meromorphic order is invariant under a period.** If `f` is periodic\nunder `c`, then `ord_{z+c} f = ord_z f`. Hence the order — the local\nzero/pole multiplicity — is a lattice-periodic function of the base point. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mathlib.EllipticDivisorDegree.fundamentalParallelogram","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mathlib.EllipticDivisorDegree.fundamentalParallelogram","kind":"def","line":120,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/EllipticDivisorDegree.lean#L120-L126","doc":"**The half-open fundamental parallelogram** `{ s·ω₁ + t·ω₂ : s, t ∈ [0,1) }`\nof the lattice `⟨ω₁, ω₂⟩`. A single such cell contains exactly one representative\nof each lattice coset (the `Int.fract` reduction of\n`QOU.MathlibExt.DoublyPeriodicLiouville.const_of_doublyPeriodic`), so summing a\nlattice-periodic divisor over it is the \"degree over a fundamental domain\". ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mathlib.EllipticDivisorDegree.isElliptic_logDeriv","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mathlib.EllipticDivisorDegree.isElliptic_logDeriv","kind":"theorem","line":111,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/EllipticDivisorDegree.lean#L111-L118","doc":"**The logarithmic derivative of an elliptic function is elliptic** (periodic\nunder both periods). This is *exactly* the reason the contour integral of\n`logDeriv f` over `∂P` vanishes: opposite edges of the parallelogram are period\ntranslates, so the elliptic (= doubly-periodic) integrand cancels. It is the\nformalizable half of the `Σ ord = (1/2πi)∮ f'/f = 0` route. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mathlib.EllipticDivisorDegree.IsElliptic.periodic₂","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mathlib.EllipticDivisorDegree.IsElliptic.periodic₂","kind":"theorem","line":109,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/EllipticDivisorDegree.lean#L109-L109","doc":"`f` is periodic under the second period. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mathlib.EllipticDivisorDegree.IsElliptic.periodic₁","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mathlib.EllipticDivisorDegree.IsElliptic.periodic₁","kind":"theorem","line":107,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/EllipticDivisorDegree.lean#L107-L107","doc":"`f` is periodic under the first period. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mathlib.EllipticDivisorDegree.IsElliptic.meromorphic","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mathlib.EllipticDivisorDegree.IsElliptic.meromorphic","kind":"theorem","line":105,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/EllipticDivisorDegree.lean#L105-L105","doc":"`f` is meromorphic on the whole plane. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mathlib.EllipticDivisorDegree.IsElliptic.mk","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mathlib.EllipticDivisorDegree.IsElliptic.mk","kind":"ctor","line":103,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/EllipticDivisorDegree.lean#L103-L103","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mathlib.EllipticDivisorDegree.IsElliptic","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mathlib.EllipticDivisorDegree.IsElliptic","kind":"structure","line":99,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/EllipticDivisorDegree.lean#L99-L109","doc":"**An elliptic function**: meromorphic on all of `ℂ` and doubly periodic under\n`ω₁, ω₂`. (`ℝ`-independence of the periods — so the lattice is genuinely\nrank-2 — is carried as a separate hypothesis where needed, matching\n`QOU.MathlibExt.DoublyPeriodicLiouville`.) ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mathlib.EllipticDivisorDegree.periodic_logDeriv","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mathlib.EllipticDivisorDegree.periodic_logDeriv","kind":"theorem","line":86,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/EllipticDivisorDegree.lean#L86-L95","doc":"**The logarithmic derivative `f'/f` of a periodic function is periodic.**\nSince `logDeriv f = deriv f / f` and both `deriv f` and `f` are periodic, so is\ntheir quotient. On the two edges of `∂P` that differ by the period `c`, the\n`logDeriv f` integrand agrees; with opposite orientation the contributions cancel\n— the boundary-integral cancellation that drives the degree-zero proof. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mathlib.EllipticDivisorDegree.periodic_deriv","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mathlib.EllipticDivisorDegree.periodic_deriv","kind":"theorem","line":74,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/EllipticDivisorDegree.lean#L74-L84","doc":"**The derivative of a periodic function is periodic.** If `f (·+c) = f`, then\n`deriv f (·+c) = deriv f`. This is the pointwise statement that makes the two\nhorizontal (resp. vertical) edges of a fundamental parallelogram carry the same\n`f'/f` integrand up to the period shift. ","signatureChunk":0}]}